## 1. Objective and scope

Determine why teams following the same nominal workplace process have persistent differences in end-to-end completion time across sites.

Treat the process as a distributed software system:

- The documented process is the specification.
- Each site’s implementation is a build or configuration.
- Work items are test inputs.
- Process steps, handoffs, tools, and policies are components.
- Completion time and quality are observable outputs.

The study will:

1. Verify that the cross-site timing difference is real and persistent.
2. Test whether sites execute equivalent processes under comparable workloads.
3. Localize delays to specific steps, queues, handoffs, or exceptions.
4. Discriminate among competing explanations:
   - different input mix or complexity;
   - staffing, capacity, or scheduling differences;
   - local deviations from the nominal process;
   - tool, infrastructure, or integration performance;
   - coordination and handoff latency;
   - rework and quality failures;
   - measurement or timestamp artifacts;
   - unobserved site-level conditions.
5. Estimate which supported explanations account for a meaningful share of the difference without assuming that association alone establishes causation.

Primary outcomes are completion-time distributions and specification-compliant completion. Quality, rework, safety, and downstream defects are guardrails; faster but defective completion will not count as improvement.

## 2. Assumptions

- Sites share a formally comparable nominal process, although actual implementations may differ.
- A work item has a defensible start condition, completion condition, and stable identifier.
- Event logs, records, or prospective observations can reconstruct process paths with adequate accuracy.
- Work items can be characterized by complexity, type, priority, arrival time, and relevant dependencies.
- Completion times may be right-skewed, censored, clustered, and affected by queueing; means alone are inadequate.
- Teams may interact, share infrastructure, or route work across sites. Such dependencies must be measured rather than assuming independence.
- No deliberate operational intervention is required initially. Any later controlled test must be preapproved, low-risk, and must not withhold necessary resources or protections.
- Employee-level reporting will be avoided where possible. Findings will be reported at an aggregate level and used to evaluate systems and processes, not rank individuals.
- The observation window must cover ordinary demand variation, peak periods, staffing cycles, and enough completed items to estimate site and pathway effects reliably.

## 3. Research design

### A. Preregister the process specification and hypotheses

Before inspecting comparative results, define:

- required steps and permitted step order;
- optional branches and exception paths;
- start and completion criteria;
- expected state transitions;
- service-level targets;
- quality and compliance invariants;
- allowed local configuration differences;
- primary and secondary outcomes;
- exclusion and censoring rules;
- candidate explanations and distinguishing predictions.

Construct an executable process model or state machine. From it, derive automated conformance checks analogous to unit, integration, performance, and regression tests.

### B. Establish the timing difference

Use a retrospective cohort covering multiple operational cycles, followed by prospective replication.

For each site, estimate:

- median, geometric mean where appropriate, and selected completion-time quantiles;
- survival curves and probability of completion by fixed deadlines;
- temporal stability of differences by week or month;
- quality-adjusted completion rates.

Require persistence across time windows and relevant work categories. Check that results do not depend on one extreme period, small subgroup, or arbitrary endpoint definition.

### C. Validate observability and measurement

Run “test-oracle” checks before explanatory analysis:

- compare system timestamps with an independent sample of direct observations or audit records;
- verify clock synchronization, time zones, daylight-saving handling, and batch timestamp behavior;
- test event ordering and missing-event rates;
- identify records reopened after nominal completion;
- determine whether sites pause, reset, or close timers differently;
- reconcile duplicate, split, merged, transferred, and canceled items.

If measurement systems differ materially, harmonize definitions or model the discrepancy explicitly before comparing performance.

### D. Perform differential process testing

Replay reconstructed item histories against the process model and compare sites on:

- missing, repeated, or out-of-order steps;
- frequencies of optional and exception branches;
- unauthorized shortcuts or added approvals;
- handoff count;
- queue residence at each state;
- active processing time versus waiting time;
- rework loops and reopening;
- local tool versions, configurations, and integration paths.

Use matched “trace diffs”: compare work items with similar observable inputs but different sites, identifying the earliest stage where their trajectories diverge.

### E. Test competing explanations

Use a common set of predefined hypotheses and falsifiable predictions.

| Explanation | Expected observations if supported | Observations arguing against it |
|---|---|---|
| Input mix/complexity | Site gap shrinks after matching or adjustment; within matched categories, times converge | Large residual site gap among equivalent inputs |
| Capacity or scheduling | Delays rise with utilization, backlog, absences, shift boundaries, or batch schedules; mostly queue time | Gap persists at low utilization and during comparable staffing |
| Process deviation | Slower sites show added steps, altered ordering, more approvals, or different branching; affected paths explain delay | High conformance and similar paths across sites |
| Tools/infrastructure | Step latency aligns with system response, outages, version, configuration, or integration errors | Similar technical performance despite different completion times |
| Handoffs/coordination | Delay concentrates between owners, functions, or time zones; more handoffs predict longer times | Similar handoff structure and negligible transfer queues |
| Rework/quality | Slower sites show more failed checks, reopenings, corrections, or upstream defects | Similar first-pass yield and rework duration |
| Local policies/incentives | Priority, batching, authorization, or target rules predict site differences | Common policies are implemented equivalently |
| Measurement artifact | Gap changes materially under harmonized timestamps or validated endpoints | Independently observed times reproduce the gap |
| Learning/experience | Timing improves with tenure, repetition, or cumulative volume; composition explains site gap | Gap remains within experience strata |
| Omitted site conditions | Stable residual site effects remain after measured factors, with no localized mechanism | Measured mechanisms account for most of the gap |

Avoid adjusting for variables downstream of site assignment when estimating the total site difference. Use them separately for mechanism analysis.

### F. Quasi-experimental and controlled confirmation

Where naturally occurring changes exist, use interrupted time-series or difference-in-differences designs for:

- tool upgrades;
- staffing changes;
- policy standardization;
- training rollout;
- integration outages or repairs;
- workflow configuration changes.

Check parallel pretrends, anticipation, concurrent changes, and spillovers.

If uncertainty remains and operations permit, pre-specify limited “A/B-style” confirmation tests, such as:

- deploying a validated configuration to randomly selected comparable teams or time blocks;
- randomizing the timing of nonessential workflow aids;
- using a stepped-wedge rollout of a standardized handoff protocol.

Stratify by site and workload. Preserve all quality controls. Do not randomize harmful conditions or deliberately degrade infrastructure.

### G. Replication and negative controls

- Replicate findings in a held-out period.
- Test unaffected process steps as negative-control outcomes.
- Use exposures that should not affect timing as negative-control exposures where credible.
- Run placebo intervention dates for quasi-experimental analyses.
- Confirm that identified mechanisms predict future delays, not only historical data.

## 4. Data or evidence needed

For each work item:

- stable item and site identifiers;
- item type, complexity, priority, required skills, and dependency indicators;
- arrival, assignment, event, pause, transfer, completion, reopening, and cancellation timestamps;
- complete state-transition trace;
- personnel role or team identifier at a privacy-preserving level;
- handoffs, approvals, exceptions, and escalation events;
- quality checks, defects, corrections, and downstream outcomes;
- service-level target and priority changes;
- transfer, split, merge, and censoring status.

For each site and time interval:

- staffing levels, schedules, absences, tenure, training, and skill mix;
- backlog, arrival rate, work in progress, utilization proxies, and queue discipline;
- local policies, approval thresholds, batching rules, and operating hours;
- tool versions, configurations, permissions, integrations, infrastructure capacity, incidents, and response metrics;
- holidays, outages, demand shocks, and other contextual events;
- records of process, staffing, policy, and software changes.

Evidence for specification and validation:

- official process documentation;
- local implementation documents;
- interviews or structured observations focused on workflow facts;
- audit samples comparing logs with actual execution;
- independently timed samples for critical steps;
- change-control and deployment records.

Define a minimum data-quality threshold in advance, including acceptable rates of missing timestamps, unresolved identifier errors, and clock discrepancies.

## 5. Measurement

### Primary outcome

End-to-end elapsed time from a common valid start state to specification-compliant completion.

Report the full distribution, emphasizing:

- median;
- 75th, 90th, and 95th percentiles;
- probability of meeting target time;
- time-to-event estimates that retain right-censored items.

### Secondary outcomes

- active processing time;
- total queue and pause time;
- step-level latency;
- handoff latency and count;
- first-pass yield;
- rework count and duration;
- exception-path frequency;
- throughput and work in progress;
- specification-conformance rate;
- downstream defect, complaint, or reversal rate.

### Derived software-testing metrics

- unit-test failures: step-specific rule or latency violations;
- integration-test failures: handoff, interface, or data-transfer problems;
- conformance-test failures: invalid state transitions or missing required steps;
- performance-test failures: latency beyond prespecified thresholds under comparable load;
- regression failures: deterioration after a change relative to baseline;
- trace divergence point: first state at which matched items follow different paths or experience materially different waiting times.

### Measurement safeguards

- Use common time-zone and calendar conventions.
- Distinguish wall-clock time from business-hours time.
- Define pauses and nonworking periods consistently.
- Keep canceled and transferred cases visible; do not silently exclude them.
- Quantify missingness and timestamp validity by site.
- Assess inter-rater agreement for manually coded complexity or process deviations.
- Blind manual adjudicators to site performance where practical.
- Conduct sensitivity analyses using alternate defensible start and completion definitions.

## 6. Analysis

### A. Descriptive and localization analysis

- Plot site-level completion-time distributions and survival curves over time.
- Decompose end-to-end time into active work, queueing, handoffs, rework, and pauses.
- Use process-mining conformance analysis to identify paths and transition violations.
- Compare earliest trace divergence points for matched items.
- Examine latency by load, shift, day, item type, and process version.

### B. Risk adjustment and matching

Estimate site differences using multilevel time-to-event models or accelerated failure-time models with work items nested within teams and sites. Include predetermined input characteristics, demand conditions, and calendar effects.

Supplement with:

- exact or propensity-score matching on item type, complexity, priority, and arrival conditions;
- within-category comparisons;
- overlap diagnostics;
- estimates restricted to common-support populations.

Report both unadjusted and adjusted differences. Do not interpret an adjusted residual as causal unless identification assumptions are credible.

### C. Mechanism analysis

For each candidate mechanism:

1. Test whether it differs by site.
2. Test whether it predicts completion time under comparable inputs.
3. Test whether the timing and direction match the stated hypothesis.
4. Estimate how much of the site gap is statistically associated with that mechanism.
5. Check whether the mechanism replicates prospectively or after a relevant change.

Use causal diagrams to classify variables as baseline confounders, mediators, colliders, or outcomes. Treat mediation estimates cautiously where mediator-outcome confounding is plausible.

### D. Queueing and capacity analysis

Model arrival rates, service rates, work in progress, and utilization by site and period. Test for nonlinear increases in waiting time near capacity limits. Separate slower service from insufficient capacity and batch-release behavior.

### E. Change-based causal analysis

For natural or controlled changes:

- estimate event-study effects before and after implementation;
- include site and time fixed effects where appropriate;
- use cluster-robust or randomization-based uncertainty estimates;
- test pretrends and spillovers;
- evaluate effects on quality guardrails as well as speed.

### F. Robustness and falsification

- Repeat analyses with alternative timing definitions.
- Include censored cases using appropriate survival methods.
- Assess sensitivity to missing data and unmeasured confounding.
- Perform leave-one-period and leave-one-site-out analyses.
- Correct or hierarchically model multiple comparisons.
- Compare preregistered results with exploratory findings, labeling them separately.
- Require out-of-sample replication for any explanation used for operational decisions.

## 7. Decision / stopping rule

An explanation will be classified as **supported** only if all of the following hold:

1. The site difference is reproducible with validated measurements.
2. The hypothesized factor differs across sites in the required direction.
3. It temporally precedes the relevant delay.
4. The delay localizes to the process component predicted by the explanation.
5. The association persists among comparable work items and under prespecified robustness checks.
6. At least one stronger design—prospective replication, natural experiment, controlled rollout, or independently validated observation—supports the same conclusion.
7. No quality or compliance guardrail shows that apparent speed reflects premature or defective completion.

An explanation will be classified as:

- **Contradicted** when its distinguishing predictions repeatedly fail with adequate precision.
- **Inconclusive** when data quality, overlap, power, or identification is insufficient.
- **Partially supported** when it explains specific pathways, periods, or item classes but not the overall gap.

Before data collection, define a smallest operationally meaningful effect for both total completion time and mechanism-specific latency. Stop additional observation when:

- the planned minimum observation window is complete;
- all sites meet preregistered data-quality thresholds;
- estimates for the primary site gaps and leading mechanisms are precise enough that their confidence or credible intervals are narrower than the meaningful-effect threshold;
- results replicate in a held-out period; and
- additional data are unlikely to change the support classification.

If no candidate explanation meets the support criteria, conclude that the persistent difference is established but not explained. Do not select the largest association by default; revise instrumentation or hypotheses and preregister a follow-up study.

## 8. Limitations

- “Same nominal process” may conceal substantive differences that are difficult to observe or formalize.
- Complexity and urgency may be imperfectly measured, leaving residual case-mix confounding.
- Process logs record system interactions, not all offline work, informal coordination, or workarounds.
- Site, culture, language, labor rules, infrastructure, and demand may be inseparable in observational comparisons.
- Matching and regression cannot establish exchangeability when sites have little common support.
- Process variables are often mediators; controlling for them can obscure total effects or introduce bias.
- Natural experiments may coincide with other changes, and controlled rollouts may generate spillovers or behavior changes.
- Queueing relationships can be dynamic and nonlinear, limiting simple attribution.
- Instrumentation may alter behavior, especially during direct observation.
- Findings may not generalize to new sites, workloads, process versions, or demand regimes.
- Faster completion may shift costs or defects downstream; the study therefore depends on sufficiently long follow-up and quality guardrails.
- Privacy constraints may require aggregation that limits analysis of staffing, learning, or coordination mechanisms.

